+/*
+ * Stained Glass OS: on Windows every keyboard layout (HKL) has its own
+ * tables (kbd*.dll), and MapVirtualKeyEx, VkKeyScanEx, ToUnicodeEx and the
+ * keys a program receives follow the layout that is active, whatever the
+ * hardware. winex11 answered all of them from the X keyboard map, so a
+ * program that activated French while X had a US map got US answers.
+ *
+ * When the HKL asks for a layout the X map is not, its tables are built
+ * here from the XKB description of that layout (xkbcommon, the same data
+ * the X server compiles its maps from) and handed to win32u, which then
+ * answers from them as it does for the Wayland driver. The X map still
+ * serves the session's own layout: the user's default HKL, and any HKL of
+ * the X map's language. Virtual keys follow the Windows layouts: a key gets
+ * the letter it types; other keys keep their US virtual key, except the
+ * French and German OEM keys, which Windows assigns its own way.
+ */
+
+#if 0
+#pragma makedep unix
+#endif
+
